<details>
<summary><strong>high</strong> lone-surrogate-output: initials: initials returns a string with a lone surrogate</summary>

Every string argument is well formed UTF-16, but the return value contains an unpaired surrogate (U+D83D at index 0). A lone surrogate renders as a replacement character, makes encodeURIComponent throw and is changed by TextEncoder and by JSON round trips through UTF-8. It usually means a string was cut between the two halves of a surrogate pair, for example by slice, substring, substr or a length limit counted in UTF-16 code units.

Expected: A well formed string. Cut text at code point or grapheme boundaries so surrogate pairs stay together.

Minimal input: `'\u{1F600}'`

Confidence: high, confirmed in a fresh process

Reproduction:

```js
// initials returns a string with a lone surrogate
// Found by breakhound. Package: buggy-lib@1.0.0
import { initials } from 'buggy-lib';

const result = initials('\u{1F600}');
console.log(result);
// true means the string contains a lone surrogate (isWellFormed() is false on Node 20+):
console.log(/[\uD800-\uDBFF](?![\uDC00-\uDFFF])|(?<![\uD800-\uDBFF])[\uDC00-\uDFFF]/.test(result));
```

Vitest test:

```ts
import { expect, test } from 'vitest';
import { initials } from 'buggy-lib';

const LONE_SURROGATE = /[\uD800-\uDBFF](?![\uDC00-\uDFFF])|(?<![\uD800-\uDBFF])[\uDC00-\uDFFF]/;

test("initials returns well formed UTF-16", () => {
  const result = initials('\u{1F600}');
  expect(result).not.toMatch(LONE_SURROGATE);
});
```

</details>

